Good First Contributions — VM Provisioning Scripts
===================================================

Welcome! This file lists concrete, self-contained improvements to the VM
provisioning scripts under scripts/. They are ideal starting points if you
want to contribute to Phantom Tracker but are not sure where to begin.

Each item is scoped to a small number of files, requires no prior knowledge
of the eBPF or kernel parts of the project, and has a clear acceptance
criterion. Shell scripting experience is sufficient for all of them.

If you pick one up, open an issue to let others know you are working on it.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Security
--------

[ ] Hash the VM console password instead of storing it in plaintext
    File:  scripts/lib/cloudinit.sh
    Why:   The --password argument is currently written as plain text into the
           seed ISO inside the 'plain_text_passwd' cloud-init field. Anyone
           with read access to the ISO can recover the password.
    How:   Use 'openssl passwd -6 <password>' to produce a SHA-512 hash and
           write it to 'hashed_passwd' instead. The hash should be computed in
           create_vm.sh before calling cloudinit_make_iso, so the plaintext
           never touches the ISO.
    Skill: bash, openssl, cloud-init user-data format

[ ] Restrict read permissions on the seed ISO
    File:  scripts/lib/cloudinit.sh (cloudinit_make_iso)
    Why:   The seed ISO is created with default umask permissions, so other
           users on the same host can read it and extract the password.
    How:   Add 'chmod 600 "$iso"' immediately after xorriso writes the file.
    Skill: bash, Linux file permissions

[ ] Replace SLIRP networking with a host bridge
    File:  scripts/create_vm.sh
    Why:   The current '-net user' option uses QEMU's built-in SLIRP stack.
           It is slow, provides no isolation between VMs, and does not support
           inbound connections without explicit port forwarding.
    How:   Create a TAP interface per VM and attach it to a Linux bridge
           (ip tuntap, ip link, brctl or 'ip link set ... master'). Replace
           the '-net user' flags in QEMU_CMD with '-netdev tap,...' and
           '-device virtio-net-pci,...'. Add a corresponding teardown path.
    Skill: bash, Linux networking (bridge, TAP), iproute2

