Scalability
-----------

[ ] Allocate SSH port automatically instead of defaulting to 2222
    File:  scripts/create_vm.sh, scripts/lib/args.sh
    Why:   If two VMs are started with the default --ssh-port, the second QEMU
           process fails silently because port 2222 is already in use.
    How:   When --ssh-port is not provided by the user, scan for a free port
           starting from 2222 using 'ss -tlnp' or by attempting to bind.
           Print the chosen port clearly so the user knows how to connect.
    Skill: bash, ss/netstat, port allocation

[ ] Add a VM inventory file
    File:  scripts/create_vm.sh (new helper, e.g. scripts/lib/inventory.sh)
    Why:   After creating several VMs there is no record of which VMs exist,
           what port they are on, or where their disks are. Every management
           operation requires the user to remember or guess.
    How:   On successful VM creation, append a record to a file such as
           ~/.phantom-tracker/vms (JSON or TSV). Each record should include:
           name, disk path, seed ISO path, SSH port, NUMA socket, creation
           date. Provide a companion list_vms.sh script that prints the
           inventory in a readable table.
    Skill: bash, JSON or text file handling

[ ] Manage vsock CID allocation across multiple VMs
    File:  scripts/create_vm.sh, scripts/lib/inventory.sh (if implemented)
    Why:   vhost-vsock requires a unique guest-cid per VM on the same host.
           CIDs 0 (hypervisor) and 1 (local) are reserved by the kernel;
           CIDs 2 and 3 are also typically reserved. Currently the --vsock-cid
           argument defaults to 4 for all VMs, so launching a second VM
           without an explicit --vsock-cid will result in a QEMU error or
           silent failure.
    How:   Track allocated CIDs in the VM inventory file. On VM creation,
           if --vsock-cid is not provided, pick the lowest free CID >= 4 that
           is not already in use by a running VM (check /dev/vhost-vsock and
           the inventory). Release the CID when the VM is destroyed.
   Skill: bash, vsock/vhost-vsock, VM inventory

[ ] Add cgroup memory limits to match libvirt's resource isolation
    File:  scripts/create_vm.sh
    Why:   The per-VM systemd scope currently only isolates CPU time. Without
           memory limits, VMs compete for host RAM through the kernel's page
           reclaim with no per-VM ceiling. libvirt sets three properties on
           every scope: MemoryHigh (soft limit, triggers reclaim early),
           MemoryMax (hard OOM kill ceiling), and MemorySwapMax=0 (no swap).
    How:   Parse --mem (e.g. "4G") into MiB. Add to the systemd-run call:
             --property="MemoryHigh=$((mem_mib + 256))M"
             --property="MemoryMax=$((mem_mib + 512))M"
             --property="MemorySwapMax=0"
           The 256M/512M overhead constants match libvirt's defaults and cover
           QEMU process memory (vCPU threads, device emulation, QEMU heap).
    Skill: bash, cgroup v2, systemd-run --property
